\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{What the corner character can detect}

\p{For a #{d}-simplex or #{d}-cube with #{d\geq3}, the
\vocabk{local corner groups}{frap-000Z} have rotation subgroup #{A_d}.
Their contribution to the \vocabk{total-orientation character}{frap-000S}
passes through #{(A_d)_{\mathrm{ab}}}. The
\vocabk{abelianization}{frap-000S} \vocabk{calculation}{frap-0010}
therefore measures exactly what this detector can see, as in
\citet{Section 4.1, p. 11, and equation (19), p. 14}{marchetti2025rubik}.}

\p{In dimension three the target is #{C_3}, the familiar corner
twist sum. In dimension four the local group has grown to the
nonabelian #{A_4}, but the character still sees only a #{C_3}
quotient: every local change in the Klein four group disappears
from it. In dimensions five and above the local group is perfect,
so this corner character is identically trivial on the ambient
framed transports.}

\p{A lost detector does not classify the legal moves. Other piece
types, permutation signs and the subgroup actually generated by
facet turns may still impose constraints. Even when the target is
nontrivial, [conservation](frap-000T) requires a generator check;
the local abelianization alone does not supply one.}
